Description: AIMS OS — AIMS Senegal lab workstation profile (IT remote access)
 Profile for the fixed workstations of the AIMS Senegal (Mbour) computer
 lab. NOT shipped on the ISO: it is installed by hand on the golden
 machine before the FOG capture, so it only ever lands on cloned lab
 machines, never on a student laptop installed from the ISO.
 .
 Ships remote administration for the IT team:
 .
  * NTP time sync enabled (systemd-timesyncd), a Kerberos prerequisite
    for the FreeIPA directory (ipa.lab.aimssnportal.org).
  * openssh-server, restricted by a drop-in under
    /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/: public key only (password and
    keyboard-interactive refused), root login refused, and only the
    aimsit account allowed in.
  * the aimsit account (created at install time, password locked,
    hidden from the GDM user list) with passwordless sudo, so the team
    can administer a machine over SSH with the "AIMS IT labo" key kept
    in the AIMS 1Password vault.
  * the authorized key under /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d/aimsit. Rotate
    it by shipping a new version of this package.
  * hardening against the keyboard: GRUB superuser (hash set on the
    golden machine with aims-labo-grub-password, normal boot stays
    password-free), the installer's local sudo account locked, and
    NetworkManager reserved to administrators (polkit).
  * no suspend: GNOME idle suspend off by default (dconf) and the
    systemd sleep targets masked, so the machine stays reachable.
  * the lab software set on top of aims-os-everything: VLC, Scilab,
    QGIS, Flatpak (GeoGebra), QEMU/KVM with GNOME Boxes and virt-manager
    (student-owned VMs, session mode, /dev/kvm granted to the seated
    user), rootless Podman with pre-pulled database images and the
    aims-db helper. Vendor software (VS Code, Google Chrome, Zotero,
    Bruno, draw.io, Julia, Ghidra, Android Studio + SDK, GeoGebra) is
    fetched by aims-labo-extras on the golden machine.
  * a UFW rule opening port 22 to the campus LAN (192.168.10.0/24)
    only, since aims-os-core enables UFW with deny-in. Removed when
    the package is removed.
